H("CHAPTER 4",16,WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER)
H("RESULTS AND DISCUSSION",14,WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER)
NL()

H("4.1  Case Study Configuration")
P("To evaluate the proposed framework, a case study was conducted for the location Greater Noida, India, using the developed frontend-backend analysis platform. The geographical location was selected through the user interface, and the simulation date was fixed as 13 March 2026 in order to obtain location-specific solar irradiance data for that day. A parametric sweep was performed over a range of geometric configurations as described below.")
P("The module mounting height was varied from 50 cm to 450 cm with increments of 50 cm, while the tilt angle was varied from 10 degrees to 50 degrees in steps of 10 degrees. The azimuth angle of the PV module was fixed at 180 degrees for south-facing panels, which is the standard orientation for photovoltaic installations in the northern hemisphere to maximize solar exposure. The bifaciality factor was assumed to be 0.7, which is a practical value for modern bifacial modules. The ground surface type was selected as concrete, corresponding to an albedo value of 0.3. The Nominal Operating Cell Temperature (NOCT) was maintained at 45 degrees Celsius.")
P("The irradiance was fetched using the NASA POWER API, and the observed total GHI for the selected date was 6.08 kWh/m2 with a peak GHI of 866.53 Wh/m2. These values are consistent with the expected solar resource availability for the Greater Noida region during the month of March, confirming the reliability of the data retrieval process.")

H("4.2  Combined Parametric Sweep Results")
P("The parametric analyses were executed using the MATLAB engine incorporating the mathematical formulation discussed in Chapter 3. A total of 45 evaluation points (9 height levels x 5 tilt angle levels) were computed for the concrete surface (albedo = 0.3). For each evaluation point, the framework calculated the total energy output, peak power, and rear gain percentage.")
P("The optimal configuration among the 45 evaluation points was identified based on the maximum energy optimization objective. The results indicated an optimal tilt angle of 30 degrees and a mounting height of 450 cm for the selected albedo value of 0.3 (concrete surface). At this optimal configuration, the rear gain was computed to be 16.55% using equation (11). This result demonstrates that a properly configured bifacial PV installation at the selected location can achieve substantial additional energy generation from the rear surface compared to an equivalent monofacial installation.")

H("4.3  I-V and P-V Characteristics")
P("The current-voltage (I-V) and power-voltage (P-V) characteristics were plotted for the various parameter-based configurations analyzed in the sweep. The I-V curves illustrate the variation in short-circuit current and open-circuit voltage across different installation configurations, reflecting the changes in effective irradiance received by the bifacial module under each scenario.")
P("The P-V curves clearly show the shift in maximum power point (MPP) as the installation parameters are varied. Configurations with higher effective irradiance (resulting from optimal tilt, height, and albedo combinations) exhibit higher peak power values on the P-V curve. The graphical representation of these characteristics provides a visual confirmation of the parametric analysis results and enables the system designer to assess the electrical performance implications of different installation choices.")
P("[Figure 4.1: I-V characteristics for different configurations - Insert graph here]")
P("[Figure 4.2: P-V characteristics for different configurations - Insert graph here]")

H("4.4  Optimal Configuration: Maximum Energy")
P("Table 4.1 presents the top five configurations ranked by maximum energy output. The optimization objective in this case is to maximize the total energy generated by the bifacial PV system over the analysis period.")

