H("CHAPTER 5",16,WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER)
H("CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK",14,WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER)
NL()

H("5.1  Summary of Contributions")
P("This project has presented a comprehensive computational framework for the parametric analysis and optimization of bifacial photovoltaic systems. The key contributions of this work can be summarized as follows:")
P("1. A rigorous mathematical model for computing the front-side and rear-side irradiance on bifacial PV modules has been implemented, incorporating the Liu-Jordan isotropic sky model for front-side irradiance estimation and the Yusufoglu view factor approach for accurate rear-side irradiance calculation with shadow correction.")
P("2. A MATLAB/Simulink model has been developed for simulating the electrical characteristics (I-V and P-V curves) of bifacial PV modules under varying irradiance and temperature conditions.")
P("3. A user-oriented frontend platform has been designed and implemented that integrates location-based solar irradiance data retrieval from the NASA POWER API with automated parametric sweep analyses, providing an accessible tool for PV system designers.")
P("4. The framework supports dual optimization objectives (maximum energy and maximum rear gain), enabling system designers to evaluate trade-offs and make informed configuration decisions.")
P("5. The proposed approach has been validated through a case study for Greater Noida, India, with results showing good agreement with published literature.")

H("5.2  Key Findings")
P("The parametric analyses conducted in this study have yielded the following key findings:")
P("1. For the case study location (Greater Noida, India) with a concrete ground surface (albedo = 0.3), the optimal configuration for maximum energy output was identified as a tilt angle of 30 degrees and mounting height of 450 cm, achieving a total energy of 3.168 kWh and rear gain of 16.55%.")
P("2. The optimal configuration for maximum rear gain was a tilt angle of 10 degrees and height of 450 cm, achieving a rear gain of 17.90% but with a lower total energy of 2.994 kWh.")
P("3. Ground surface albedo was confirmed as the most influential parameter affecting rear-side irradiance contribution, with rear gain varying from below 5% for low-albedo surfaces to over 24% for highly reflective surfaces such as aluminum.")
P("4. Mounting height has a positive but diminishing effect on rear-side performance, consistent with the near-logarithmic dependence reported in the literature.")
P("5. The interaction between tilt angle, height, and albedo necessitates combined parametric optimization rather than independent single-parameter studies.")

H("5.3  Limitations")
P("The present work has certain limitations that should be acknowledged. First, the mathematical model assumes uniform ground albedo beneath the module, which may not accurately represent real-world installations where ground surface properties can vary spatially. Second, the analysis is based on single-day irradiance data; extending the analysis to annual energy yield computations would provide more comprehensive optimization results. Third, the current implementation does not account for inter-row shading effects in multi-row array configurations, which can be significant in large-scale installations. Fourth, temperature effects on module performance are modeled using the simplified NOCT approach, and more detailed thermal models may improve the accuracy of energy yield predictions.")

H("5.4  Scope for Future Work")
P("Several directions for future research and development are identified based on the outcomes of this project:")
P("1. Extension to Annual Energy Yield: The current framework can be extended to perform year-round energy yield simulations by aggregating daily analyses across all days of the year, providing more comprehensive optimization guidance for long-term system design.")
P("2. Multi-Row Array Modeling: Incorporating inter-row shading models would enable the framework to optimize row-to-row spacing in addition to tilt, height, and albedo, making it applicable to utility-scale bifacial solar farm design.")
P("3. Machine Learning Integration: Developing machine learning models trained on the parametric sweep data could enable rapid prediction of optimal configurations without requiring full computational analysis, significantly reducing the optimization time for new locations.")
P("4. Economic Analysis: Integrating techno-economic analysis capabilities would allow the framework to evaluate the cost-effectiveness of different bifacial PV configurations, accounting for structural costs, ground preparation costs, and the economic value of additional energy generation.")
P("5. Real-Time Monitoring: Developing a real-time monitoring system that continuously compares actual system performance with predicted values could enable adaptive optimization and early detection of performance degradation.")
P("6. Geographic Information System Integration: Combining the optimization framework with GIS tools could enable automated site assessment for large-scale bifacial PV deployment planning, incorporating terrain, land use, and local albedo mapping data.")

